  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improving social sustainability in the granite supply chain, South India (SSF24S003)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project is a collaboration between Den Hollandsche and Stichting Local Impact Hub from the Netherlands and Granite Products Corporation and Paradigm Shift Foundation from India. It focuses on the granite quarries in three locations in South India. Each location gives a specific pattern and colour. The quarries are chosen based on these stone characteristics. Around ten quarries supply granite in this supply chain. The locations of the quarries are in Tamil Nadu and Karnataka Province. The beneficiaries of this project are the workers in the quarries and factories in the granite supply chain.The project focuses on improving working conditions in the factory and the quarries. In the quarries the risks related to working conditions are higher, also since there are quite a lot of migrant workers and contracted workers in the quarries. The project focuses on addressing the working risks and labour rights risks, in order to prevent these risks from occurring and diminishing them when present. Living wage is the third relevant sustainability issue the project focuses on. The minimum wage in India is set by the government, and all companies uphold this wage. Often quarry workers are hired through a contractor. The project partners investigate the types of contract forms in quarries and factories and the income strategies of the workers&apos; families in order to provide income improvements towards living income. Partners focus activities and interventions on the risks that are most prominent in the risk assessment.</narrative>
  </description>
